			]
			title: "&bDrones"
			x: -4.0d
			y: 6.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: [
				"3300CBEB9EA7135B"
				"3D806EF240D67C03"
				"159FB53B985A6BBD"
				"3486F30F73E53018"
			]
			description: [
				"Four systems, four philosophies, and no wrong answer:"
				""
				"&eSFM&r - write what should happen, in words."
				"&eXNet&r - declare what connects to what, in a table."
				"&eIntegrated Dynamics&r - describe the conditions, and let them drive."
				"&ePneumaticCraft&r - hand a robot a plan and let it fly."
				""
				"Most bases end up using two or three of these at once, because each is best at something the others aren't. Pipes still have their place - but you are no longer wrenching a wall every time the plan changes."
			]
			icon: "pneumaticcraft:harvesting_drone"
			id: "7083373948204BA5"
			min_required_dependencies: 1
			rewards: [
				{
					id: "73A08DBD90BF1DD5"
					type: "xp_levels"
					xp_levels: 5
				}
				{
					id: "190F0F1CB4B83E6E"
					type: "random"
				}
			]
			shape: "hexagon"
			size: 1.5d
			subtitle: "&7The base runs itself now"
			tasks: [{
				id: "3ACCE7D8B7799310"
				title: "Automate the deciding"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			title: "&b&lThe Base Thinks"
			x: 0.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["6AF9FD70EB5A15AB"]
			description: [
				"Super Factory Manager starts with the &eLabel Gun&r: you shoot inventories to give them names, then write a program that refers to those names."
				""
				"&e&lThat is the whole idea.&r Instead of routing pipes, you label a chest 'ore', label a machine 'smelter', and write &7EVERY 20 TICKS DO INPUT FROM ore OUTPUT TO smelter&r."
				""
				"&7Labels are shared across the whole manager network, and one inventory can hold several labels.&r"
				""
				"&8Label everything as you build it. Retrofitting labels onto an existing base is the tedious part."
			]
			icon: "sfm:labelgun"
			id: "607804A8645EDE2E"
			rewards: [{
				id: "29838CBBA8EED71D"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Name things, then program them"
			tasks: [{
				id: "263C67086DCBAD0C"
				item: "sfm:labelgun"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bSFM: Labels"
			x: -8.0d
			y: 3.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"The &eManager&r runs your program. The syntax is close to English and the in-game editor autocompletes it."
				""
				"&e&lA real example:&r"
				"&7EVERY 20 TICKS DO"
				"&7  INPUT 64 iron_ingot FROM storage"
				"&7  OUTPUT TO furnace_input"
				"&7END&r"
				""
				"&e&lIt handles items, fluids, energy and gases&r with the same syntax, across any distance the network reaches, with filters, priorities and round-robin built in."
				""
				"&c&lOne manager can replace an entire base's pipework.&r That is not an exaggeration - this is the single biggest quality-of-life mod in the pack."
			]
			icon: "sfm:manager"
			id: "725479E5D38351E6"
			rewards: [{
				id: "5E831686D3A6BEA7"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Plain-English automation"
			tasks: [{
				id: "378065B63A390C69"
				item: "sfm:manager"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bSFM: Programs"
			x: -6.0d
			y: 3.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"Beyond simple moves, SFM supports conditionals, redstone triggers, and resource-count checks."
				""
				"&e&lIF statements let a program react:&r only smelt when ore is above a threshold, only run the reactor when power is low, only craft when stock drops."
				""
				"&7Because the program is text, you can copy it, version it and paste it into another manager - which makes duplicating a working setup trivial.&r"
				""
				"&8The in-game documentation covers the full syntax. It is short and worth reading once."
			]
			icon: "sfm:manager"
			id: "194714DFFD141AFE"
			rewards: [{
				id: "3AA4E2EE9DB87CAD"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Conditionals and resource tracking"
			tasks: [{
				id: "7B8DA875FA248AB7"
				title: "Complete"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			title: "&bSFM: Advanced Patterns"
			x: -4.0d
			y: 3.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["6AF9FD70EB5A15AB"]
			description: [
				"An &eXNet Controller&r plus &eNetwork Cable&r gives you a single GUI that configures every connector on the network."
				""
				"&e&lThe model is channels.&r Each channel handles one job - items, fluids, energy or logic - and each connector on that channel is configured from the controller rather than by walking to it."
				""
				"&7That central configuration is XNet's advantage over pipes: changing a filter means opening one screen, not finding one block.&r"
			]
			icon: "xnet:controller"
			id: "0626ED59A561D82D"
			rewards: [{
				id: "642A2A99A83E01FA"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Channels, centrally configured"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "53D6C240B0A5AB1F"
					item: "xnet:controller"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "6E5FC9C5675B0E1A"
					item: "xnet:connector_routing"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "&bXNet: Controllers & Cables"
			x: -8.0d
			y: 4.5d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"&eConnectors&r attach to inventories and machines. Each one gets a name and a set of channel roles in the controller GUI."
				""
				"&e&lPriority and round-robin are built in&r, so splitting output between several machines evenly, or preferring one until it is full, is a dropdown rather than a build."
				""
				"&7Advanced Connectors extend range and throughput. The Wireless Router links separate XNet networks, including across dimensions.&r"
				""
				"&8SFM and XNet solve the same problem differently - SFM is a program, XNet is a control panel. Most people end up using both."
